1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:04,963
Let's start somewhere familiar,
your BrightLeaf Odoo from Video 2.

2
00:00:05,575 --> 00:00:09,098
I'm in Inventory, Products, and I'm
switching from the picture cards to

3
00:00:09,138 --> 00:00:12,675
the list view, the icon with the
little lines on the top right.

4
00:00:13,838 --> 00:00:16,976
Look at this. Rows and columns.

5
00:00:17,588 --> 00:00:19,426
Each row is one product.

6
00:00:20,038 --> 00:00:23,161
Each column is one piece of
information about that product: the

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:28,263
reference, the name, the sales price,
the cost, the quantity on hand.

8
00:00:29,432 --> 00:00:33,957
Guess what? Underneath, Odoo
stores this in a database.

9
00:00:34,570 --> 00:00:38,717
And a database, at the level we need,
is simply a super-organised Excel

10
00:00:38,757 --> 00:00:39,595
workbook.

11
00:00:40,778 --> 00:00:43,178
Four words, and you know
all of them already.

12
00:00:44,348 --> 00:00:46,848
A table is like one sheet in a workbook.

13
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:51,283
Odoo has a table for products, a
table for suppliers, a table for

14
00:00:51,323 --> 00:00:52,548
purchase orders.

15
00:00:53,726 --> 00:00:56,301
A row is one record, one thing.

16
00:00:56,914 --> 00:00:59,776
One product. One supplier.

17
00:01:00,389 --> 00:01:01,676
One stock count.

18
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:07,090
A column is one field, one type of
information that every record has.

19
00:01:07,703 --> 00:01:10,240
Product name. Cost.

20
00:01:10,852 --> 00:01:11,953
Lead time.

21
00:01:13,130 --> 00:01:16,290
And the database is the whole
workbook holding all of those tables

22
00:01:16,330 --> 00:01:17,043
together.

23
00:01:18,212 --> 00:01:20,837
Now here's something
most people never notice.

24
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:23,524
I'll open the pallet jack wheels.

25
00:01:24,137 --> 00:01:26,237
Look at the very end of the web address.

26
00:01:26,849 --> 00:01:30,997
There's a number. That number is
Odoo's own internal ID for this

27
00:01:31,037 --> 00:01:33,912
product. It never changes.

28
00:01:34,524 --> 00:01:38,422
You can rename the product, change
the price, change the supplier,

29
00:01:38,462 --> 00:01:40,200
and that number stays the same.

30
00:01:40,812 --> 00:01:44,434
Hold on to that idea, because unique
IDs are going to save us in a few

31
00:01:44,474 --> 00:01:45,212
minutes.

32
00:01:46,382 --> 00:01:49,667
So why are we using Google Sheets
as Stocky's database instead of

33
00:01:49,707 --> 00:01:52,845
something fancy? Three reasons.

34
00:01:54,008 --> 00:01:57,083
One, it's free and you
already know how to use it.

35
00:01:57,696 --> 00:02:02,355
Two, it lives online, so our
automation tool, n8n, can reach it from

36
00:02:02,396 --> 00:02:06,243
anywhere, whereas an Excel file
sitting on your laptop is locked inside

37
00:02:06,283 --> 00:02:10,768
your laptop. And three, you can
open it and see exactly what Stocky

38
00:02:10,808 --> 00:02:14,258
wrote. Nothing is hidden in a black box.

39
00:02:15,434 --> 00:02:18,257
There's an app called Airtable that
does the same job with a few more

40
00:02:18,297 --> 00:02:20,734
features, and it's a fine alternative.

41
00:02:21,347 --> 00:02:24,109
But for learning, Google
Sheets is perfect.

42
00:02:25,292 --> 00:02:29,052
My shortcut: I just typed "sheets
dot new" in the address bar,

43
00:02:29,092 --> 00:02:31,067
and a blank sheet opened instantly.

44
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:35,065
I'll click the title "Untitled
spreadsheet" and rename it "BrightLeaf

45
00:02:35,105 --> 00:02:36,430
Stocky Database".
